 triangles is a **necessary** condition for $P$ and $Q$ having the same area"] [:db/cas 17592186454638 :answer-field/key "II" "$P$ and $Q$ having the same area is a **necessary** condition for $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles"] [:db/cas 17592186454639 :answer-field/key "III" "$P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles is a **sufficient** condition for $P$ and $Q$ having the same area"] [:db/cas 17592186454640 :answer-field/key "IV" "$P$ and $Q$ having the same area is a **sufficient** condition for $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles"] [:db/cas 17592186420702 :question/problem "$\\,$ $a\\ge 10$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n- **I.** $a=10$\n- **II.** $a>15$\n- **III.** $a>5$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$\\,$ $a\\ge 10$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186420702 :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a=10)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a=10,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition II is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>15)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a>15,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>5)\\,⇏\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $a=7,$ then we have $a≱10.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition $a=10$ is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a=10)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a=10,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition $a>15$ is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>15)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a>15,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition $a>5$ is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>5)\\,⇏\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $a=7,$ then we have $a≱10.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $a=10$; $a>15$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186475192 :answer-field/key "I" "$a=10$"] [:db/cas 17592186475193 :answer-field/key "II" "$a>15$"] [:db/cas 17592186475194 :answer-field/key "III" "$a>5$"] [:db/cas 17592186420703 :question/problem "$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n- **I.** $x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0$\n- **II.** $x\\le 2$\n- **III.** $x\\ge 0$\n\nSelect all that apply." "$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186420703 :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given inequality is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x({x}^{2}+x-6)=6≰0.$\n\n- Condition II is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,(x\\le 2)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEvery solution of ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$ satisfies $x\\le 2.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x\\ge 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x≱0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given inequality is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition $x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0$ is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x({x}^{2}+x-6)=6≰0.$\n\n- Condition $x\\le 2$ is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,(x\\le 2)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEvery solution of ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$ satisfies $x\\le 2.$\n\n- Condition $x\\ge 0$ is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x\\ge 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x≱0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x\\le 2$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186475259 :answer-field/key "I" "$x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0$"] [:db/cas 17592186475260 :answer-field/key "II" "$x\\le 2$"] [:db/cas 17592186475261 :answer-field/key "III" "$x\\ge 0$"] [:db/cas 17592186420704 :question/problem "$\\,$ $18∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n- **I.** $2∣x$ and $9∣x$\n- **II.** $3∣x$ and $6∣x$\n- **III.** $72∣x$\n\nSelect all that apply." "$\\,$ $18∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186420704 :question/worked-solution "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $18$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $2$ and $9.$\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇔\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇍\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }6∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=6$ is divisible by $3$ and by $6$ but not divisible by $18.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇏\\,(72∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=18$ is divisible by $18$ but not divisible by $72.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition “$2∣x$ and $9∣x$” is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $18$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $2$ and $9.$\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇔\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition “$3∣x$ and $6∣x$” is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇍\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }6∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=6$ is divisible by $3$ and by $6$ but not divisible by $18.$\n\n- Condition $72∣x$ is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇏\\,(72∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=18$ is divisible by $18$ but not divisible by $72.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$2∣x$ and $9∣x$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186475265 :answer-field/key "I" "$2∣x$ and $9∣x$"] [:db/cas 17592186475266 :answer-field/key "II" "$3∣x$ and $6∣x$"] [:db/cas 17592186475267 :answer-field/key "III" "$72∣x$"] [:db/cas 17592186420705 :question/problem "$\\,$ If $A⊂B,$ then $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the implication above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $A⊂B$\n- **II.** $A⊂B$ is a **necessary** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$\n- **III.** $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A⊂B$\n- **IV.** $A⊂B$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$" "$\\,$ If $A⊂B,$ then $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the implication above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186420705 :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(\\,A⊂B\\,)}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,\\underbrace{(\\,A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅\\,)}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement I is true. $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $A⊂B.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. $A⊂B$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(\\,A⊂B\\,)}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,\\underbrace{(\\,A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅\\,)}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement “$A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $A⊂B$” is true. $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $A⊂B.$\n\n- Statement “$A⊂B$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$” is true. $A⊂B$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $A⊂B$”; “$A⊂B$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186454574 :answer-field/key "I" "$A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $A⊂B$"] [:db/cas 17592186454575 :answer-field/key "II" "$A⊂B$ is a **necessary** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$"] [:db/cas 17592186454576 :answer-field/key "III" "$A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A⊂B$"] [:db/cas 17592186454577 :answer-field/key "IV" "$A⊂B$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$"] [:db/cas 17592186420910 :question/problem "![](images/28/28a7eb32cfcd800c907e9f4af927eca7bd8616d36162682413db62a9feabefbb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/28/28a7eb32cfcd800c907e9f4af927eca7bd8616d36162682413db62a9feabefbb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186420910 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fecf59674259d41222ffd981b1ab6d90c474fe2b0e37a6d5f7626f34536a6896.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/56/56a7fc09ed2a2a15b0949fa6041dc9c0ee6beb69f30b1a5c048afd8c57887d52.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative” and “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” are false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fecf59674259d41222ffd981b1ab6d90c474fe2b0e37a6d5f7626f34536a6896.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/56/56a7fc09ed2a2a15b0949fa6041dc9c0ee6beb69f30b1a5c048afd8c57887d52.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” only."] [:db/cas 17592186475068 :answer-field/key "I" "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative"] [:db/cas 17592186475069 :answer-field/key "II" "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero"] [:db/cas 17592186475070 :answer-field/key "III" "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive"] [:db/cas 17592186420911 :question/problem "![](images/01/01d8d85064253b9241b1fd93c22381897f23556d6e02c5c2c0161c94cee3bbdf.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/01/01d8d85064253b9241b1fd93c22381897f23556d6e02c5c2c0161c94cee3bbdf.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186420911 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *in* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb4fd4068760bb9132052b26508f0e1e0c02c45d425770de804b17ce7c512919.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/46/46afd4cde01c95388c9daefbef0668ef696d5817ab9ea3614982edf58a9bdf55.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statem