 of $F$ across $C$ is zero"] [:db/cas 17592186475072 :answer-field/key "II" "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive"] [:db/cas 17592186475073 :answer-field/key "III" "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive"] [:db/cas 17592186420914 :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1d25942ae680c068b587f38bfbc968c8850b8fa2db2003e994e1ec32dad892.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/0f/0f1d25942ae680c068b587f38bfbc968c8850b8fa2db2003e994e1ec32dad892.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186420914 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7acc3028de66ae71ae4e425da639e51a357a77f2b0a345290592cac91ba17d3.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/04/04639a0074aa4607fa57bfe589aad1780f8da6f573d8589a68c2fdb569b5a129.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7acc3028de66ae71ae4e425da639e51a357a77f2b0a345290592cac91ba17d3.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/04/04639a0074aa4607fa57bfe589aad1780f8da6f573d8589a68c2fdb569b5a129.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” only."] [:db/cas 17592186475074 :answer-field/key "I" "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero"] [:db/cas 17592186475075 :answer-field/key "II" "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive"] [:db/cas 17592186475076 :answer-field/key "III" "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative"] [:db/cas 17592186420985 :question/problem "If $A=\\{-2,2\\}$ and $B=\\{c,d\\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times B?$\n\nI. $(-2,d,c)$\n\nII. $(-2,(c,d))$\n\nIII. $((2,d),c)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "If $A=\\{-2,2\\}$ and $B=\\{c,d\\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times B?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186420985 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${-2,2}$ with ${c,d},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/98/98e7cfca515290b952ea5aefc9b2516a5cd6a68de0dc048c2075128e6c60b651.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(-2,c),(-2,d),(2,c),(2,d)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times B\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/1e/1e96aeb1c5bfb1f9faa590a8eb8b01528a1ce9431a3f1ceac92b156a9bdde221.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times B|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |B|=4\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((2,d),c)\\in (A\\times B)\\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${-2,2}$ with ${c,d},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/98/98e7cfca515290b952ea5aefc9b2516a5cd6a68de0dc048c2075128e6c60b651.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(-2,c),(-2,d),(2,c),(2,d)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times B\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/1e/1e96aeb1c5bfb1f9faa590a8eb8b01528a1ce9431a3f1ceac92b156a9bdde221.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times B|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |B|=4\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((2,d),c)\\in (A\\times B)\\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $((2,d),c)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186475204 :answer-field/key "I" "$(-2,d,c)$"] [:db/cas 17592186475205 :answer-field/key "II" "$(-2,(c,d))$"] [:db/cas 17592186475206 :answer-field/key "III" "$((2,d),c)$"] [:db/cas 17592186420986 :question/problem "For the 