{x\\text{ is composite}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x\\text{ is composite,}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{provided that}\\,\\underbrace{4\\,|\\,x}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Given that the implication $P⇒Q$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- Whenever $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n\n- $Q,$ provided that $P$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option “Whenever $4\\,|\\,x,$ then $x$ is composite” reads\n\n$$\n\\text{Whenever}\\,\\underbrace{4\\,|\\,x}_{P(x)},\\,\\text{then}\\,\\underbrace{x\\text{ is composite}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option “$4\\,|\\,x$ whenever $x$ is composite” reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{4\\,|\\,x}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{whenever}\\,\\underbrace{x\\text{ is composite}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option “$x$ is composite, provided that $4\\,|\\,x$” reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x\\text{ is composite,}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{provided that}\\,\\underbrace{4\\,|\\,x}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Whenever $4\\,|\\,x,$ then $x$ is composite”; “$x$ is composite, provided that $4\\,|\\,x$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186475227 :answer-field/key "I" "Whenever $4\\,|\\,x,$ then $x$ is composite"] [:db/cas 17592186475228 :answer-field/key "II" "$4\\,|\\,x$ whenever $x$ is composite"] [:db/cas 17592186475229 :answer-field/key "III" "$x$ is composite, provided that $4\\,|\\,x$"] [:db/cas 17592186421149 :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x=0$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}=x$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n- **I.** Whenever $x=0,$ then ${x}^{2}=x$\n- **II.** ${x}^{2}=x$ whenever $x=0$\n- **III.** $x=0,$ provided that ${x}^{2}=x$\n\nSelect all that apply." "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x=0$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}=x$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186421149 :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $P⇒Q$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- Whenever $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n\n- $Q,$ provided that $P$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n\\text{Whenever}\\,\\underbrace{x=0\\text{,}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{then}\\,\\underbrace{{x}^{2}=x}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}=x}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{whenever}\\,\\underbrace{x=0}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x=0\\text{,}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{provided that}\\,\\underbrace{{x}^{2}=x}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and II only.\"" "Given that the implication $P⇒Q$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- Whenever $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n\n- $Q,$ provided that $P$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option “Whenever $x=0,$ then ${x}^{2}=x$” reads\n\n$$\n\\text{Whenever}\\,\\underbrace{x=0\\text{,}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{then}\\,\\underbrace{{x}^{2}=x}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option “${x}^{2}=x$ whenever $x=0$” reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}=x}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{whenever}\\,\\underbrace{x=0}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option “$x=0,$ provided that ${x}^{2}=x$” reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x=0\\text{,}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{provided that}\\,\\underbrace{{x}^{2}=x}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Whenever $x=0,$ then ${x}^{2}=x$”; “${x}^{2}=x$ whenever $x=0$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186475230 :answer-field/key "I" "Whenever $x=0,$ then ${x}^{2}=x$"] [:db/cas 17592186475231 :answer-field/key "II" "${x}^{2}=x$ whenever $x=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186475232 :answer-field/key "III" "$x=0,$ provided that ${x}^{2}=x$"] [:db/cas 17592186421311 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **II.** ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **III.** The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186421311 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2)-x(2x)+{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2)-x(2x)+{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [:db/cas 17592186475304 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186475305 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186475306 :answer-field/key "III" "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186421404 :question/problem "Which of the following are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n- **I.** $A⊄B$\n- **II.** $|A|\\ne |B|$\n- **III.** $A⊇B$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the following are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186475239 :answer-field/key "I" "$A⊄B$"] [:db/cas 17592186475240 :answer-field/key "II" "$|A|\\ne |B|$"] [:db/cas 17592186475241 :answer-field/key "III" "$A⊇B$"] [:db/cas 17592186421405 :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $R?$\n- **I.** $x\\,R\\,y$ if $xy>0$\n- **II.** $x\\,R\\,y$ if $x=2y$\n- **III.** $x\\,R\\,y$ if $x-y$ is a rational number\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186421405 :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $0\\cdot 0=0≯0.$\n\n- Relation II is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric because $2=2(1)$ but $1\\ne 2(2).$\n\n- Relation III is an equivalence relation:\n\n- It is reflexive because for all $x\\in R,$ we have $x-x=0$ is a rational number.\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $x,y\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ is a rational number, then $y-x=-(x-y)=-r$ is also a rational number.\n\n- It is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ and $y-z=q,$ where $r,q$ are rational numbers, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=(x-y)+(y-z)=r+q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation “$x\\,R\\,y$ if $xy>0$” is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $0\\cdot 0=0≯0.$\n\n- Relation “$x\\,R\\,y$ if $x=2y$” is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric because $2=2(1)$ but $1\\ne 2(2).$\n\n- Relation “$x\\,R\\,y$ if $x-y$ is a rational number” is an equivalence relation:\n\n- It is reflexive because for all $x\\in R,$ we have $x-x=0$ is a rational number.\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $x,y\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ is a rational number, then $y-x=-(x-y)=-r$ is also a rational number.\n\n- It is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ and $y-z=q,$ where $r,q$ are rational numbers, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=(x-y)+(y-z)=r+q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$x\\,R\\,y$ if $x-y$ is a rational number” only."] [:db/cas 17592186475358 :answer-field/key "I" "$x\\,R\\,y$ if $xy>0$"] [:db/cas 17592186475359 :answer-field/key "II" "$x\\,R\\,y$ if $x=2y$"] [:db/cas 17592186475360 :answer-field/key "III" "$x\\,R\\,y$ if $x-y$ is a rational number"] [:db/cas 17592186421406 :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $P(A),$ where $A={1,2,3,…,10}.$ \n\n$$\nX\\,R\\,Y\\,⇔\\,|X|=|Y|\n$$\n\n Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R$ is reflexive\n- **II.** $R$ is symmetric\n- **III.** $R$ is transitive\n- **IV.** $R$ is an equivalence relation" "Consider the following relation defined on the set $P(A),$ where $A={1,2,3,…,10}.$ \n\n$$\nX\\,R\\,Y\\,⇔\\,|X|=|Y|\n$$\n\n Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186421406 :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive. For all $X\\in P(A),$ $|X|\\,=\\,|X|.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric. For all $X,Y\\in P(A),$ if $|X|\\,=\\,|Y|,$ then $|Y|=|X|.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive. For all $X,Y,Z\\in P(A),$ if $|X|=|Y|$ and $|Y|=|Z|,$ then $|X|=|Z|.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV only.\"" "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive. For all $X\\in P(A),$ $|X|\\,=\\,|X|.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric. For all $X,Y\\in P(A),$ if $|X|\\,=\\,|Y|,$ then $|Y|=|X|.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive. For all $X,Y,Z\\in P(A),$ if $|X|=|Y|$ and $|Y|=|Z|,$ then $|X|=|Z|.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$R$ is reflexive”; “$R$ is symmetric”; “$R$ is transitive”; “$R$ is an equivalence relation” only."] [:db/cas 17592186454621 :answer-field/key "I" "$R$ is reflexive"] [:db/cas 17592186454622 :answer-field/key "II" "$R$ is symmetric"] [:db/cas 17592186454623 :answer-field/key "III" "$R$ is transitive"] [:db/cas 17592186454624 :answer-field/key "IV" "$R$ is an equivalence relation"] [:db/cas 17592186421408 :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n- **I.** $A\\,R\\,B$ if $A\\cap B=∅$\n- **II.** $A⊃B$\n- **III.** $A=B$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186421408 :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}\\cap {1}\\ne ∅.$\n\n- Relation II is also not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}⊅{1}.$\n\n- Relation III is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation “$A\\,R\\,B$ if $A\\cap B=∅$” is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}\\cap {1}\\ne ∅.$\n\n- Relation $A⊃B$ is also not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}⊅{1}.$\n\n- Relation $A=B$ is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A=B$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186475242 :answer-field/key "I" "$A\\,R\\,B$ if $A\\cap B=∅$"] [:db/cas 17592186475243 :answer-field/key "II" "$A⊃B$"] [:db/cas 17592186475244 :answer-field/key "III" "$A=B$"] [:db/cas 17592186421409 :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={∗,⋆,∘,⋄}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(∗,∗),(⋆,⋆),(∘,∘),(⋄,⋄),(∗,⋆),(⋆,∗)}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R$ is reflexive\n- **II.** $R$ is symmetric\n- **III.** $R$ is transitive\n- **IV.** $R$ is an equivalence relation" "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={∗,⋆,∘,⋄}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(∗,∗),(⋆,⋆),(∘,∘),(⋄,⋄),(∗,⋆),(⋆,∗)}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186421409 :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive because $(∗,∗),(⋆,⋆),(∘,∘),(⋄,⋄)\\in R.$$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x)\\in R$ whenever $(x,y)\\in R.$$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in {∗,⋆,∘,⋄},$ if  $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,z)\\in R,$  then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Recall that it's sufficient to check only the cases when both pairs have different components: \n\n- $(∗,⋆)\\in R$ and $(⋆,∗)\\in R,$ and also $(∗,∗)\\in R$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $(⋆,∗)\\in R$ and $(∗,⋆)\\in R,$ and also $(⋆,⋆)\\in R$$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Since $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it is an equivalence relation.$\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\"" "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive because $(∗,∗),(⋆,⋆),(∘,∘),(⋄,⋄)\\in R.$$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x)\\in R$ whenever $(x,y)\\in R.$$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in {∗,⋆,∘,⋄},$ if  $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,z)\\in R,$  then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Recall that it's sufficient to check only the cases when both pairs have different components: \n\n- $(∗,⋆)\\in R$ and $(⋆,∗)\\in R,$ and also $(∗,∗)\\in R$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $(⋆,∗)\\in R$ and $(∗,⋆)\\in R,$ and also $(⋆,⋆)\\in R$$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Since $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it is an equivalence relation.$\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$R$ is reflexive”; “$R$ is symmetric”; “$R$ is transitive”; “$R$ is an equivalence relation”."] [:db/cas 17592186454629 :answer-field/key "I" "$R$ is reflexive"] [:db/cas 17592186454630 :answer-field/key "II" "$R$ is symmetric"] [:db/cas 17592186454631 :answer-field/key "III" "$R$ is transitive"] [:db/cas 17592186454632 :answer-field/key "IV" "$R$ is an equivalence relation"] [:db/cas 17592186421410 :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),(\\b