 consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral $3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{3z-1}}{{e}^{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t$ matches the definition. We use a substitution $s={t}^{3},$ then $3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t=\\text{d}s.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-1}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3(z-1)+2}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3(z-1)}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}(3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{s}^{z-1}{e}^{-s}\\,\\text{d}s=Γ(z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Integral $3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-3}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t$ does *not* match the definition. The integrand ${t}^{3z-3}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\n- Integral $3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$ does *not* match the definition. The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{3z-1}}{{e}^{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186475041 :answer-field/key "I" "$3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{3z-1}}{{e}^{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t$"] [:db/cas 17592186475042 :answer-field/key "II" "$3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-3}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t$"] [:db/cas 17592186475043 :answer-field/key "III" "$3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$"] [:db/cas 17592186432784 :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7?$\n\nI. $276$\n\nII. $647$\n\nIII. $805$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186432784 :question/worked-solution "To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $276,$ we have that \n\n$$\n27-2(6)=27-12=15\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $276$ is not divisible 