},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{11}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression $max({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{11},\\mu )$ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression $min({X}_{2},{X}_{4},…,{X}_{10},\\sigma )$ is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{11}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression $\\frac{{X}_{1}+{X}_{11}}{{\\mu }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+1}$ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $min({X}_{2},{X}_{4},…,{X}_{10},\\sigma )$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186475125 :answer-field/key "I" "$max({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{11},\\mu )$"] [:db/cas 17592186475126 :answer-field/key "II" "$min({X}_{2},{X}_{4},…,{X}_{10},\\sigma )$"] [:db/cas 17592186475127 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\frac{{X}_{1}+{X}_{11}}{{\\mu }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+1}$"] [:db/cas 17592186434508 :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{X}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=6}^{10}{X}_{i}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\sqrt{{X}_{i}^{2}-{\\sigma }^{2}}$\n\nIII. $\\frac{{X}_{1}+{X}_{10}}{2}+\\mu $\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186434508 :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{X}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=6}^{10}{X}_{i}$ is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ only.\n\n- Expression ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\sqrt{{X}_{i}^{2}-{\\sigma }^{2}}$ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\n- Expression $\\frac{{X}_{1}+{X}_{10}}{2}+\\mu $ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{X}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=6}^{10}{X}_{i}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186475104 :answer-field/key "I" "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{X}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=6}^{10}{X}_{i}$"] [:db/cas 17592186475105 :answer-field/key "II" "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\sqrt{{X}_{i}^{2}-{\\sigma }^{2}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186475106 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\frac{{X}_{1}+{X}_{10}}{2}+\\mu $"] [:db/cas 17592186434510 :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both  known, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}|{X}_{i}-\\mu |$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}({X}_{i}+\\sigma )$\n\nIII. $min({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12})$\n\nSelect all that apply." "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both  known, which of the following are statistics?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186434510 :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ and the *known* population mean $\\mu $ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only.\n\n- Expression III is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}|{X}_{i}-\\mu |$ is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ and the *known* population mean $\\mu $ only.\n\n- Expression ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}({X}_{i}+\\sigma )$ is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only.\n\n- Expression $min({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12})$ is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}|{X}_{i}-\\mu |$; ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}({X}_{i}+\\sigma )$; $min({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12})$."] [:db/cas 17592186475128 :answer-field/key "I" "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}|{X}_{i}-\\mu |$"] [:db/cas 17592186475129 :answer-field/key "II" "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}({X}_{i}+\\sigma )$"] [:db/cas 17592186475130 :answer-field/key "III" "$min({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12})$"] [:db/cas 17592186434595 :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=2\\}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186434595 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=3x$ since $0=3\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=2$ since $0\\ne 2.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=3x$ since $0=3\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=2$ since $0\\ne 2.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=0\\}$; $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x\\}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186475331 :answer-field/key "I" "$A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=0\\}$"] [:db/cas 17592186475332 :answer-field/key "II" "$B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x\\}$"] [:db/cas 17592186475333 :answer-field/key "III" "$C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=2\\}$"] [:db/cas 17592186434754 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈5+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=8$\n\nII. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local maximum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈5+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186434754 :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=8$” is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite” is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a local maximum of $f$” is true. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=8$”; “$a$ is a local maximum of $f$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186475156 :answer-field/key "I" "${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=8$"] [:db/cas 17592186475157 :answer-field/key "II" "$A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186475158 :answer-field/key "III" "$a$ is a local maximum of $f$"] [:db/cas 17592186434755 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=3$\n\nII. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a saddle point of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186434755 :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(2)-1(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=3$” is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(2)-1(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite” is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a saddle point of $f$” is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=3$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186475159 :answer-field/key "I" "${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=3$"] [:db/cas 17592186475160 :answer-field/key "II" "$A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186475161 :answer-field/key "III" "$a$ is a saddle point of $f$"] [:db/cas 17592186434759 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)=4$\n\nII. $A$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local maximum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186434759 :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=1$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)=4$” is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=1$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is positive-definite” is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a local maximum of $f$” is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)=4$”; “$A$ is positive-definite” only."] [:db/cas 17592186475153 :answer-field/key "I" "${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)=4$"] [:db/cas 17592186475154 :answer-field/key "II" "$A$ is positive-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186475155 :answer-field/key "III" "$a$ is a local maximum of $f$"] [:db/cas 17592186434764 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=-15$\n\nII. $A$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186434764 :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(3)-4(4)+2(-6) \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-19<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-31<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=-15$” is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(3)-4(4)+2(-6) \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is negative-definite” is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-19<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-31<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a local minimum of $f$” is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186475162 :answer-field/key "I" "${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=-15$"] [:db/cas 17592186475163 :answer-field/key "II" "$A$ is negative-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186475164 :answer-field/key "III" "$a$ is a local minimum of $f$"] [:db/cas 17592186434983 :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}12{x}^{2}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(-1,0)>0$\n\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0$\n\n- **III.** The point $(-1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}12{x}^{2}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186434983 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,0)=det({f}^{″}(-1,0))=8\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)=({f}^{″}(-1,0))_{11}=8>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(-1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0,$ the point $(-1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $H(-1,0)>0$ is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,0)=det({f}^{″}(-1,0))=8\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0$ is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)=({f}^{″}(-1,0))_{11}=8>0.$\n\n- Statement “The point $(-1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$” is false. Since $H(-1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0,$ the point $(-1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $H(-1,0)>0$; $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186454693 :answer-field/key "I" "$H(-1,0)>0$"] [:db/cas 17592186454694 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0$"] [:db/cas 17592186454695 :answer-field/key "III" "The point $(-1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$"] [:db/cas 17592186435053 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(6-{x}^{3})-({x}^{2}+4)$\n\nII. $(3{x}^{3}+x)\\cdot (2{x}^{4}+4)$\n\nIII. $(1-3{x}^{3})÷(3x)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186435053 :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-3{x}^{3}}{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3x}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression $(6-{x}^{3})-({x}^{2}+4)$ is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression $(3{x}^{3}+x)\\cdot (2{x}^{4}+4)$ gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression $(1-3{x}^{3})÷(3x)$ is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-3{x}^{3}}{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3x}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(6-{x}^{3})-({x}^{2}+4)$; $(3{x}^{3}+x)\\cdot (2{x}^{4}+4)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186475119 :answer-field/key "I" "$(6-{x}^{3})-({x}^{2}+4)$"] [:db/cas 17592186475120 :answer-field/key "II" "$(3{x}^{3}+x)\\cdot (2{x}^{4}+4)$"] [:db/cas 17592186475121 :answer-field/key "III" "$(1-3{x}^{3})÷(3x)$"] [:db/ensure 17592186415476 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473960 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473130 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473131 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473961 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473132 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473133 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473962 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473134 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473135 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186415479 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186454702 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186453904 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186453905 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186454703 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186453906 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186453907 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186454704 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186453908 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186453909 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186415620 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473862 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186472934 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186472935 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473863 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186472936 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186472937 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186473864 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186472938 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186472939 :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186415621 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592